2026-08-24 - step-007: Simple threshold detector design decisions
- Warmup=100, n_std=3.0 defaults: longer warmup than EWMA/Z-score (10/30) because the band is frozen forever after warmup -- a bigger sample gives a more reliable one-shot mean/std estimate.
- Binary score (0.0/1.0), not tail-probability: this is the deliberate "dumb baseline" contrast against the other 3 detectors' continuous [0,1] scores, per the plan's "simple threshold detector" framing.
- Never re-estimates the band after warmup (unlike WindowedGaussian's step-buffer recompute, EWMA's continuous decay, or Z-score's rolling window) -- this is the defining trait, proven directly by test_never_adapts_unlike_zscore.
- 14 new tests (111/111 total green). No new dependencies.
2026-08-25 step-008: Per-profile threshold sweep tabulation for all 4 statistical detectors. Finding: ThresholdDetector optimizes to 'never fire' (NAB 0.0, threshold above max score) on every profile -- static frozen bands generate FP storms after level shifts, so the sweep prefers silence. EWMA/Z-score also collapse to 0.0 on reward_low_fp. Gaussian leaderboard top at 40.13 standard, exactly reproducing step-004 result (regression cross-check).
2026-08-27 step-009: isolation forest in NEW module src/iforest.py (kept detectors.py statistical-only). Design: shingle_size=4 (stream-to-vector), train_size=256 / retrain_interval=256 (drift adaptation, bounded memory), n_trees=64 psi=128 vs paper 100/256 -- halved for pure-Python runtime; ensemble converges well before 100 trees (Liu 2008 Fig.5). Seeded random.Random for byte-identical reruns. Warmup convention matches other detectors (score 0.0 until first fit).
